From 2c0b7bf32132c41e86cef21f02638efdf736a8ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ferdinand Vanmaele <info@fvanmaele.de>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 03:50:34 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] [clangd] Race the index shard against the AST for
 navigation

The first version answered from the shard synchronously and fell back to the
AST when the merged index did not know the symbol yet. At startup the
background index loads its stored shards asynchronously, so a go-to-definition
sent right after opening a file found nothing and waited for the AST anyway.

Now, when the shard records a symbol at the cursor, a task polls the index
(50 ms steps, up to 5 s) while the AST path runs as before; whichever answers
first delivers the result, once. locateIndexedSymbol and
findReferencesToIndexedSymbol return std::nullopt while the index does not
know the symbol, which is what the polling waits on.

Measured through eglot on a two-file project including deal.II and Boost,
second session (stored index): first go-to-definition 6623 ms -> 606 ms, same
result; ClangdTests 1409/1409 pass.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
